  "updated": "2026-08-22T17:37:58.698415+00:00",
  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 AM EDT Saturday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Showers and some embedded storms are likely today, \nespecially near the Chesapeake Bay and on the Eastern Shore. While \nsevere weather is not expected, localized flash flooding will be \npossible. \n\nA weak stationary boundary remains just south of the forecast area \nthis morning, roughly aligned along the NC/SC coast. There is also a \nbroad trough axis aloft across most of the eastern CONUS. Locally, \nthere is a ribbon of mostly light rain across the \nnorthern/northwest portions of the area. This conflicts with several \nearlier runs of various CAMS which depicted rather widespread \novernight showers and storms, particularly across SE VA and NE NC. \nIn fact, these areas are actually quite stable as of this writing \nwith sfc temps in the lower 70s and dew points in the upper 60s, \nwhich yields low/near-zero sfc-based and MUCAPE. Any \nappreciable instability remains confined to our S, in closer \nproximity to the front. Therefore, really don't see much \nconvective activity developing through around sunrise. \n\nThe front will (eventually) lift northward as we head into this \nafternoon. Model and WPC progs also depict weak low pressure \ndeveloping in the vicinity of the Chesapeake Bay and Eastern Shore. \nThis, combined with some low-level convergence, should be sufficient \nfor showers to develop in these areas by late this afternoon and \nafternoon. Instability looks quite meager, especially early on in \nthe day, so am not expecting widespread thunderstorms at this time. \nA moist atmospheric column (PWATS ~2.1\" across the E/SE) and slow-\nmoving activity will support a continued heavy rain and localized \nflash flooding risk. The 00z HREF also depicted a relatively \nimpressive signal for heavy rain and some flash flooding in the \nEastern Shore vicinity. However, all of the 00z members that \ncomprised the ensemble suite are currently handling the ongoing \nactivity extremely poorly. Therefore, confidence in the evolution of \nconvection later today is low and some recent runs of the CAMs are \nless bullish about the overall coverage of showers and the\nassociated heavy rain signal. In summary, localized pockets of \nvery heavy rain are possible, but the exact location is \nuncertain and the coverage may tend to be on the low side. All \nof this precludes the need for any Flood Watch at this time. \nLate this evening, convection from the Great Lakes and OH \nValley, associated with the primary cold front and trough, could\napproach our Piedmont counties. However, diminishing \ninstability and reduced forcing with eastward extent should \nallow for most of this activity to diminish as it moves eastward\ntonight. \n\nOutside of any precip, temps will be quite \"cool\" for mid-late \nAugust and only in the upper 70s-lower 80s (some mid 80s possible \nSE). Skies will average mostly cloudy, with some clearing possible W \nof I-95 this afternoon.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures Sunday \nthrough Tuesday. Isolated to scattered diurnally driven convection \nreturns by the middle to latter part of the week.\n\nThe weak front will finally move offshore late Sunday, as the main \nupper level trough axis shifts offshore and surface high pressure \nbuilds into the SE CONUS. The surface high will usher in a drier \nairmass with seasonable temperatures in the 80s and dew points in \nthe 60s Sunday into early next week. A reinforcing intrusion of dry \nair is likely Monday night/Tuesday with dew points in the 50s \nlooking likely (except 60s far SE). The high is progged to break\ndown by Wed/Thu, with unsettled conditions and isolated- \nscattered aftn/evening convection returning mid to late week.",
